1
00:00:04,652 --> 00:00:08,828
Lesson four: financial management
and budget intelligence.

2
00:00:09,852 --> 00:00:14,100
In this lesson, we'll follow the
money: how budgets are allocated,

3
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:17,980
how spending is recorded, and how
additional funds are requested,

4
00:00:17,990 --> 00:00:19,679
reviewed and decided.

5
00:00:21,619 --> 00:00:24,981
Every shilling allocated to a
project should be traceable:

6
00:00:24,991 --> 00:00:28,067
from the original budget,
to each recorded expense,

7
00:00:28,077 --> 00:00:31,615
to each additional request,
and the decision taken on it.

8
00:00:32,652 --> 00:00:35,526
The platform supports that in three ways.

9
00:00:36,027 --> 00:00:40,828
Controlled allocations, because every
project starts with a defined budget.

10
00:00:41,885 --> 00:00:46,842
Recorded expenditure, with an item,
amount, date and receipt reference.

11
00:00:47,885 --> 00:00:52,810
And documented decisions, because every
additional fund request carries a reason,

12
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:55,759
an outcome, and a record of who decided it.

13
00:00:57,419 --> 00:00:59,831
Let's start with how the
numbers fit together,

14
00:00:59,841 --> 00:01:02,949
using the KISUMU_NITA
project as our example.

15
00:01:03,985 --> 00:01:09,255
The project was created with an
allocated budget of KES 500,000.

16
00:01:10,285 --> 00:01:14,995
An expense of KES 70,000 was
recorded for mast mounting.

17
00:01:16,052 --> 00:01:20,202
No additional funds have been
approved yet, so that line is zero.

18
00:01:21,252 --> 00:01:25,780
That leaves a remaining
balance of KES 430,000.

19
00:01:26,819 --> 00:01:31,353
An important detail: in this system,
approved additional funds are counted as

20
00:01:31,363 --> 00:01:34,498
outflow, alongside recorded expenditure.

21
00:01:34,998 --> 00:01:39,825
Pending requests are shown separately, and
aren't deducted until they're approved.

22
00:01:41,319 --> 00:01:44,597
Administrators open
Financials from the sidebar.

23
00:01:45,652 --> 00:01:49,073
Four headline figures
summarise the whole portfolio.

24
00:01:49,573 --> 00:01:52,799
Total Budget is the sum
of all project budgets.

25
00:01:53,852 --> 00:01:58,640
Total Outflow is recorded expenditure,
plus approved additional funds.

26
00:01:59,685 --> 00:02:02,624
Remaining is what's left after all outflow.

27
00:02:03,125 --> 00:02:07,815
And Pending Requests shows the value of
fund requests still awaiting a decision,

28
00:02:07,825 --> 00:02:09,631
which are not yet deducted.

29
00:02:10,685 --> 00:02:13,220
Below the figures are the analytics.

30
00:02:13,721 --> 00:02:17,969
Monthly Spend Trend shows recorded
expenditure over the last six months,

31
00:02:17,979 --> 00:02:20,488
so you can see when money is being spent.

32
00:02:21,519 --> 00:02:26,294
Expense Categories groups spending by the
first word of each expense description.

33
00:02:26,795 --> 00:02:31,733
Here, the KES 70,000 for Mast
Mounting appears under Mast

34
00:02:31,743 --> 00:02:35,541
— which is why clear, consistent
expense descriptions matter.

35
00:02:36,952 --> 00:02:41,122
Budget versus Outflow per
Project shows, for each project,

36
00:02:41,132 --> 00:02:46,180
how much has gone out, in orange,
and how much remains, in green.

37
00:02:47,219 --> 00:02:50,738
Cumulative Spend adds up
recorded expenditure over time,

38
00:02:50,748 --> 00:02:54,091
so the line rises as each
new expense is recorded.

39
00:02:55,152 --> 00:02:58,990
Top Spending Projects ranks
projects by total outflow.

40
00:02:59,490 --> 00:03:03,438
Fund Request Status shows the
value of requests that are pending,

41
00:03:03,448 --> 00:03:09,070
approved or rejected, and it stays empty
until the first request is decided or made.

42
00:03:10,119 --> 00:03:14,159
At the bottom, the Per-Project
Breakdown table puts it all together:

43
00:03:14,169 --> 00:03:18,091
budget, spent, approved
funds, remaining balance,

44
00:03:18,101 --> 00:03:19,855
and the percentage used.

45
00:03:20,885 --> 00:03:23,831
Usage above eighty per
cent is shown in orange,

46
00:03:23,841 --> 00:03:26,181
and above one hundred per cent in red.

47
00:03:26,681 --> 00:03:30,063
Selecting any row opens
that project's detail.

48
00:03:31,485 --> 00:03:36,677
Expenditure is recorded by administrators,
from the Financials tab of a project.

49
00:03:37,719 --> 00:03:42,475
Select Add, then enter an item description,
the amount in Kenyan shillings,

50
00:03:42,485 --> 00:03:45,359
the date, and a receipt or reference number.

51
00:03:46,419 --> 00:03:51,949
In our example, the item is
Mast Mounting, for KES 70,000.

52
00:03:52,985 --> 00:03:57,545
Always include a receipt or reference, so
that every entry can be traced back to its

53
00:03:57,555 --> 00:03:59,023
supporting document.

54
00:04:00,052 --> 00:04:04,404
When you select Add, the expense appears
in the project's expenditure table,

55
00:04:04,414 --> 00:04:09,580
the remaining balance is reduced immediately,
and the entry is recorded in the audit log.

56
00:04:10,985 --> 00:04:14,849
Now let's look at additional
funding, from the team leader's side.

57
00:04:15,349 --> 00:04:18,282
When a project needs money
beyond its allocation,

58
00:04:18,292 --> 00:04:22,364
the team leader opens Fund
Requests and selects Request Funds.

59
00:04:23,385 --> 00:04:27,704
If they manage more than one project,
they first choose the project.

60
00:04:28,205 --> 00:04:32,603
The form then shows the project's
name and SMP reference at the top.

61
00:04:33,619 --> 00:04:36,682
Enter the amount required,
in Kenyan shillings,

62
00:04:36,692 --> 00:04:38,394
and explain the reason.

63
00:04:38,895 --> 00:04:40,479
Both are required.

64
00:04:41,519 --> 00:04:44,908
A good reason is specific:
what the money is for,

65
00:04:44,918 --> 00:04:49,158
why it wasn't covered by the original
budget, and what will happen without it.

66
00:04:50,219 --> 00:04:52,273
Select Submit Request.

67
00:04:52,773 --> 00:04:56,683
The request is saved as pending,
it's recorded in the audit log,

68
00:04:56,693 --> 00:05:00,920
and the administrator receives an
in-app notification and an SMS.

69
00:05:02,319 --> 00:05:06,378
On the administrator's side, the
request is visible straight away.

70
00:05:06,878 --> 00:05:09,655
The dashboard shows a Needs
your attention banner,

71
00:05:09,665 --> 00:05:15,039
the Financials menu shows a badge, and the
notification bell shows an unread alert.

72
00:05:16,085 --> 00:05:21,453
On the Financials page, Pending
Requests now shows KES 50,000,

73
00:05:21,463 --> 00:05:24,643
and the request appears in the
Pending Fund Requests list,

74
00:05:24,653 --> 00:05:29,311
with the amount, the project, the
reason, who requested it, and when.

75
00:05:30,352 --> 00:05:32,861
The administrator has two choices.

76
00:05:33,362 --> 00:05:36,015
Approve asks for the amount to approve.

77
00:05:36,513 --> 00:05:40,188
It's pre-filled with the amount
requested, but it can be adjusted

78
00:05:40,198 --> 00:05:43,957
— for example, if only part
of the request is justified.

79
00:05:44,985 --> 00:05:49,331
Decline asks for an optional reason,
which is shared with the team leader.

80
00:05:50,352 --> 00:05:54,743
Either way, the decision is recorded with
the administrator's name and the time,

81
00:05:54,753 --> 00:05:59,444
the audit log is updated, and the team
leader receives an in-app notification and an

82
00:05:59,454 --> 00:06:01,299
SMS with the outcome.

83
00:06:02,352 --> 00:06:08,039
When funds are approved, the administrator
also receives an SMS confirming the release.

84
00:06:09,585 --> 00:06:14,243
Let's see the effect of
approving the KES 50,000 request.

85
00:06:15,285 --> 00:06:18,888
The budget remains KES 500,000.

86
00:06:19,389 --> 00:06:22,979
Expenditure is still KES 70,000.

87
00:06:23,477 --> 00:06:27,770
And approved additional
funds are now KES 50,000.

88
00:06:28,819 --> 00:06:33,373
So the remaining balance
falls to KES 380,000.

89
00:06:34,885 --> 00:06:37,277
The Financials page confirms it.

90
00:06:37,778 --> 00:06:44,812
Total Outflow is now KES 120,000,
Remaining is KES 380,000,

91
00:06:44,822 --> 00:06:47,462
and Pending Requests has returned to zero.

92
00:06:48,485 --> 00:06:53,462
In the breakdown table, approved
funds now show KES 50,000,

93
00:06:53,472 --> 00:06:57,323
and the budget used has risen from
fourteen to twenty-four per cent.

94
00:06:58,385 --> 00:07:02,639
And the Fund Request Status chart
now shows the approved request.

95
00:07:04,185 --> 00:07:06,552
A final word on interpretation.

96
00:07:07,052 --> 00:07:11,176
The charts and figures are only as
accurate as the information entered.

97
00:07:11,674 --> 00:07:15,824
They summarise recorded
budgets, expenses and decisions.

98
00:07:16,323 --> 00:07:20,251
They don't independently verify
that money was spent as described.

99
00:07:21,285 --> 00:07:24,986
That's why the supporting habits
matter: clear descriptions,

100
00:07:24,996 --> 00:07:28,345
receipt references, specific
reasons for requests,

101
00:07:28,355 --> 00:07:30,070
and prompt decisions.

102
00:07:31,119 --> 00:07:35,289
For the Board and project owners, the
key indicators to watch are the remaining

103
00:07:35,299 --> 00:07:39,976
balance, the percentage of budget used,
and the size and frequency of additional

104
00:07:39,986 --> 00:07:41,493
fund requests.

105
00:07:43,352 --> 00:07:47,541
To summarise: every project
begins with an allocated budget.

106
00:07:48,042 --> 00:07:51,795
Expenditure and approved additional
funds are both deducted from it,

107
00:07:51,805 --> 00:07:55,786
while pending requests are shown
separately until a decision is made.

108
00:07:56,819 --> 00:07:59,837
Team leaders request
funds with a clear reason.

109
00:08:00,337 --> 00:08:03,784
Administrators approve, adjust or decline.

110
00:08:04,282 --> 00:08:07,273
And every decision is notified and recorded.

111
00:08:08,319 --> 00:08:11,994
In the next lesson, we'll look
more closely at notifications,

112
00:08:12,004 --> 00:08:14,852
SMS messaging, and the audit trail.

113
00:08:17,819 --> 00:08:21,839
This training experience was
produced by KEYPOLE TECHNOLOGIES.
